// §A10 follow-up (§15.4 / §15.5) — `@stack_protector(...)` +
// `--no-libc`.
//
// The two pieces cover the rest of §15.5's freestanding
// profile. Together with the memcpy / memset / memmove
// intrinsics from §A10's earlier slice, they make "zero
// references to libc" a build-time guarantee — the marker
// records the contract; the canary attribute names which
// functions opt out of the canary check.
//
// `@stack_protector(.none | .strong | .all)` decorates an
// individual function:
//
//   .none    Disable canary instrumentation. The C++ side
//            lands [[gnu::no_stack_protector]] so the host
//            compiler skips inserting the guard.
//   .strong  Use the modern "strong" canary heuristic. v0.5
//            emits no per-function attribute; the toolchain-
//            wide `-fstack-protector-strong` flag is the
//            correct place to set this.
//   .all     Canary every function. Toolchain-wide concern;
//            same v0.5 emission as .strong.
//
// `--no-libc` is the v0.5 form of the manifest flag
// `profile.freestanding.no_libc = true`. The transpiler
// writes `// vestra: no_libc = true` at the top of each
// generated file; downstream link tooling can refuse to
// satisfy any unlisted external symbol, and the queued
// `vestra audit --no-libc` enumerator reads the marker to
// know which builds it should walk.

module examples.stack_protector

// A critical path function the user marked `.none` so the
// canary won't add a stack frame the kernel can't afford.
// Common pattern for ISR handlers and atomic-store-only
// helpers where the canary's read of __stack_chk_guard
// would itself be a heap touch the regime forbids.
@stack_protector(.none)
func critical_path(_ x: Int32) -> Int32 {
    return x * 2
}

// An ordinary function that should keep its canary. v0.5
// emits no per-function attribute since `.strong` is the
// compiler's default and there's no portable way to "force-
// on" a canary on one function; the audit names the
// attribute regardless.
@stack_protector(.strong)
func careful_path(_ x: Int32) -> Int32 {
    return x + 100
}
